A candidate grid and its manually copied final JSON are two separate artifacts. A spatial rule may be plausible, and the internal grid may have been checked, while a single missing/extra cell in transcription makes the submitted ARC grid malformed. Avoid that split: serialize the validated row-list directly into the one action JSON object, preferably compactly and without additional prose.

Before submission, check the exact object that will be sent:
- action has the permitted value (`submit`) and the object has the required grid field;
- grid is a list of the stated number of rows;
- every row is a list of the stated number of integer cells;
- each cell is in 0–9;
- JSON parses successfully.

If feedback says the grid was not a valid ARC grid, first audit action schema, parseability, row count, row widths, and values. Do not infer that this feedback falsifies the transformation rule. If the grid was valid but incorrect, instead reject and revise the generator against all examples. This lesson is especially important for large hand-copied grids and after repeated speculative attempts.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u059/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/exact-payload-validation.md"}
